# Build-context excludes (used by both docker and podman).
# Keeps the image slim and, importantly, prevents shipping a .py file whose
# imports aren't in requirements.txt (COPY *.py bakes in every non-ignored .py,
# and any one of them could be run inside the container via `exec`). The static
# validator (container_check.py) enforces this invariant in CI.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.git/
.gitignore
venv/
.venv/
env/
.env
*.pem
# Tests never belong in the image.
tests/
*_test.py
test_*.py
conftest.py
pytest.ini
# Docs / CI / dev config.
.github/
*.md
mcp-config.example.json

# ── Dev/test/operational tooling that imports packages NOT in requirements.txt,
# or that the app never imports at runtime. Excluding these keeps the runtime
# import graph clean. The OPERATIONAL tools you may want to `exec` inside a
# running container (openbao_setup.py, rotate_secret.py, diagnose_idp.py,
# fix_redirect_uri.py, login_flow.py, provision_dynamic_secrets.py) rely only on
# packages already in requirements.txt, so they are deliberately NOT excluded.

# container_check.py imports pyyaml (a dev-only dependency).
container_check.py
# The MCP server is a separate component with its own deps (requirements-mcp.txt:
# mcp, httpx). Shipping it in the broker image would be an unmet-import crash.
mcp_server.py
requirements-mcp.txt
# Smoke / integration / live harnesses — dev-only, not part of the runtime graph.
e2e_smoke.py
endpoint_smoke.py
integration_test_live.py
openbao_smoke.py
openbao_login_smoke.py
auth0_endpoints_live.py
auth0_pagination_live.py
